import{_ as t,o as r,c as o,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.BOeh4kYD.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"Agent Research & Competitor Intelligence · 2026-09-09","description":"Dual-source monitoring results for core Agent researchers, protocols/runtimes, and the CodeFlowMu competitor radar on 2026-09-09.","frontmatter":{"title":"Agent Research & Competitor Intelligence · 2026-09-09","description":"Dual-source monitoring results for core Agent researchers, protocols/runtimes, and the CodeFlowMu competitor radar on 2026-09-09.","outline":"deep"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-09-09.md","filePath":"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-09-09.md","lastUpdated":1788921235000}'),a={name:"en/runtime/daily-intelligence/2026-09-09.md"};function i(s,e,c,l,p,d){return r(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="agent-research-competitor-intelligence-·-2026-09-09" tabindex="-1">Agent Research &amp; Competitor Intelligence · 2026-09-09 <a class="header-anchor" href="#agent-research-competitor-intelligence-·-2026-09-09" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Agent Research &amp; Competitor Intelligence · 2026-09-09&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Only meaningful new developments are recorded. Primary sources are preferred: maintainers, official engineering posts, GitHub PRs/issues/releases, and original project documentation.</p></blockquote><h2 id="daily-takeaway" tabindex="-1">Daily takeaway <a class="header-anchor" href="#daily-takeaway" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Daily takeaway&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The strongest shared direction today is that <strong>Agent runtimes are moving the final safety check closer to the real side-effect boundary, while capability declarations, execution ownership, effect semantics, and recovery evidence are becoming explicit contracts.</strong></p><p>For CodeFlowMu/FCoP, the execution chain increasingly looks like:</p><p><strong>Capability Grant → Admission Decision → Effect Preconditions → Call-time Authority → Effect Receipt → Evidence-driven Settlement</strong>.</p><h2 id="_1-openai-codex-—-single-writer-conversations-and-identity-bound-process-control" tabindex="-1">1. OpenAI Codex — single-writer conversations and identity-bound process control <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-openai-codex-—-single-writer-conversations-and-identity-bound-process-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. OpenAI Codex — single-writer conversations and identity-bound process control&quot;">​</a></h2><p>PR #43253 makes a conversation single-writer: if an active writer already owns the conversation, another resume becomes a read-only history view instead of competing for write authority. PR #43308 moves Windows managed app-server shutdown onto a local control socket and requires the Server PID to match before drain begins. PR #43289 adds MCP <code>openai/userVerification</code> protocol handling behind an explicit client capability gate.</p><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> <code>Running</code> is not enough to describe authority. Production runtimes need distinct Writer Lease, Observer Access, Host Process Identity, and Human Verification Receipt concepts.</p><p><strong>Interaction angle:</strong> <em>Will user-verification proofs eventually be bound to the active writer/authority epoch, so a proof obtained by one conversation owner cannot survive a writer handoff?</em></p><p>Sources: <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/43253" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#43253</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/43308" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#43308</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/codex/pull/43289" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#43289</a></p><h2 id="_2-openai-agents-sdk-—-tool-names-are-not-effect-contracts" tabindex="-1">2. OpenAI Agents SDK — tool names are not effect contracts <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-openai-agents-sdk-—-tool-names-are-not-effect-contracts" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. OpenAI Agents SDK — tool names are not effect contracts&quot;">​</a></h2><p>PR #4893 reports that <code>apply_patch</code> Add File may overwrite an existing destination while still reporting <code>Created</code>; the proposed fix makes create-only semantics require proof that the destination does not exist. PR #4894 reports delimiter injection into sandbox S3 Files mount options, where one argument value can expand into an extra mount capability.</p><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> authorization cannot rely on tool names alone. It needs normalized effect classes such as create-only, overwrite, or mount-policy mutation.</p><p><strong>Status:</strong> both PRs are OPEN and are not yet released behavior.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-python/pull/4893" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#4893</a> · <a href="https://github.com/openai/openai-agents-python/pull/4894" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#4894</a></p><h2 id="_3-ag2-—-mcp-apps-introduce-an-app-only-invocation-principal" tabindex="-1">3. AG2 — MCP Apps introduce an app-only invocation principal <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-ag2-—-mcp-apps-introduce-an-app-only-invocation-principal" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. AG2 — MCP Apps introduce an app-only invocation principal&quot;">​</a></h2><p>PR #3235 is adding MCP Apps support. UI documents are resources bound to tools, and UI metadata is only delivered when the client declares rendering support. The important design point is <code>visibility=[&quot;app&quot;]</code>: an MCP App can invoke a tool that the model never sees.</p><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> the system now has at least two invocation paths—Model → Tool and Human → App UI → Tool. App-only tools need their own principal, user-gesture provenance, and authorization evidence.</p><p><strong>Status:</strong> OPEN.</p><p>Source: <a href="https://github.com/ag2ai/ag2/pull/3235" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">AG2 #3235</a></p><h2 id="_4-paperclip-—-pre-start-admission-and-plugin-capability-escalation" tabindex="-1">4. Paperclip — pre-start admission and plugin capability escalation <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-paperclip-—-pre-start-admission-and-plugin-capability-escalation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Paperclip — pre-start admission and plugin capability escalation&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Paperclip remains one of the highest-priority competitors. PR #12949 introduces a pre-start admission hook after a run is claimed but before adapter/native runner/provider side effects begin; enforcement fails closed on missing, stale, or invalid telemetry. PR #12928 treats plugin upgrades that add capabilities as <code>upgrade_pending</code> instead of silently extending the existing grant. PR #12946 records real production problems involving lease release, runaway continuation, false success classification, and incorrect cross-issue evidence.</p><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Paperclip is converging on a full runtime governance chain: Declared Capability → Approved Grant → Pre-start Admission → Run Ownership → Provider Effect → Recovery Classification.</p><p><strong>Competitor view:</strong> learning value very high; risk very high; continue treating Paperclip as a first-tier runtime control-plane competitor.</p><p><strong>Status:</strong> these PRs are still OPEN.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/12949" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#12949</a> · <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/12928" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#12928</a> · <a href="https://github.com/paperclipai/paperclip/pull/12946" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#12946</a></p><h2 id="_5-github-mcp-server-—-exact-patching-requires-proof-of-preconditions" tabindex="-1">5. GitHub MCP Server — exact patching requires proof of preconditions <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-github-mcp-server-—-exact-patching-requires-proof-of-preconditions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. GitHub MCP Server — exact patching requires proof of preconditions&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Draft PR #3232 adds <code>apply_file_patch</code>, requiring exact <code>expected_head_sha</code> and <code>expected_blob_sha</code>; patch matches must be exact, fuzzy matching and guessing are rejected, and branch updates use <code>force=false</code> so concurrent changes fail rather than overwrite newer facts.</p><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> file writes become preconditioned effects: “I expect branch X and blob Y; only if both remain true may I produce Z.” This maps directly to concurrency linearization, idempotency, and effect preconditions in FCoP 4.0.</p><p><strong>Status:</strong> DRAFT.</p><p>Source: <a href="https://github.com/github/github-mcp-server/pull/3232" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GitHub MCP Server #3232</a></p><h2 id="_6-orca-—-unknown-state-converges-only-through-positive-evidence" tabindex="-1">6. Orca — unknown state converges only through positive evidence <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-orca-—-unknown-state-converges-only-through-positive-evidence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. Orca — unknown state converges only through positive evidence&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Merged PR #19140 lets a previously <code>unknown</code> message-delivery record advance to <code>accepted</code> when a late provider echo proves the original submission arrived, without dispatching a second message. Open PR #19149 combines PID and creation-time ordering to avoid Windows PID-reuse ancestry errors. Open PR #19155 explicitly says lease expiry cannot prove an owner died; lease release requires positive root-death evidence and descendant-state evidence.</p><p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> unknown may converge through positive evidence, but timeout, lease expiry, and “process not observed” cannot manufacture facts.</p><p><strong>Competitor view:</strong> Orca&#39;s long-running, Windows, and recovery weight should keep increasing.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/pull/19140" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#19140</a> · <a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/pull/19149" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#19149</a> · <a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca/pull/19155" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#19155</a></p><h2 id="cross-project-judgment" tabindex="-1">Cross-project judgment <a class="header-anchor" href="#cross-project-judgment" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cross-project judgment&quot;">​</a></h2><blockquote><p><strong>A safe Agent system is not one that checks once and trusts forever. It repeatedly proves, closer and closer to the side-effect boundary, that the object is still the same object, the capability is still the granted capability, the owner is still the owner, and the effect preconditions still hold.</strong></p></blockquote><p>For CodeFlowMu/FCoP, six distinct stages should remain visible:</p><ol><li><strong>Capability Grant</strong></li><li><strong>Admission Decision</strong></li><li><strong>Effect Preconditions</strong></li><li><strong>Call-time Authority</strong></li><li><strong>Effect Receipt</strong></li><li><strong>Evidence-driven Settlement</strong></li></ol><p>A settlement must be allowed to remain <strong>unknown</strong> until named evidence moves it to a confirmed state; retry, timeout, or absence of evidence must not silently rewrite it as failure.</p><hr><p><strong>Monitoring scope:</strong> 41 core people + major OpenAI/Cursor/MCP/AG2/OpenHands/CrewAI project signals + 31 CodeFlowMu competitor radar entries.<br><strong>Dual-source rule:</strong> GitHub/official engineering sources plus reliably identified X/public social sources.<br><strong>Social-source result:</strong> no newly verified personal post exceeded the technical value of the primary engineering sources above, so no marketing reposts were used to fill the report.</p>',44)])])}const g=t(a,[["render",i]]);export{h as __pageData,g as default};
